WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.620
Film Courage: One great thing I just heard
you say was wherever we go, we take ourselves

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:04.620
with us.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.620
Rob Reiner: True.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.620
Wherever you are, there you are.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.790
It’s a line I remember from Buckaroo Banzai.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.580
But I’ve always loved that line because
that’s true.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.640
Film Courage: It is true and for better or
worse you can’t get away from yourself.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:15.760
Rob Reiner: You can’t!

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.210
But hopefully you want to be around yourself.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.520
That’s the key!

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.350
If you like yourself, then no matter where
you go, it’s okay.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.369
Film Courage: Right!

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.930
And I know the film’s protagonist Lyndon
struggled with various self-esteem issues

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.949
which was interesting to see [in the film]
one statement was so fascinating in that he

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:34.270
said [paraphrasing] there are show ponies
and field horses.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:39.350
Rob Reiner: Right, there are show horses and
work horses.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.750
He said he thought of himself as a work horse.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:46.600
And the reason he was is because he knew that
he was not a show horse.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.010
He was not somebody that you would immediately
take…he wasn’t overly handsome, worried

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.399
about his appeal or whatever that John or
Jack had, right?

00:00:55.399 --> 00:01:00.379
But so if he was going to be liked and in
the case of Lyndon, he was so insecure, he

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:02.160
desperately wanted to be loved.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.729
The way to make that happen was to work so
hard to deliver results for people that they

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:10.110
would love him for.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:14.920
Film Courage: You did an excellent job of
showing these various scenes where it was

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.119
unspoken but you could see what was going
on whether it was the crowd scene [as he’s

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:20.119
shaking hands and being upstaged by John].

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:24.109
When you are directing a scene, how are you
thinking about a character arc in each take?

00:01:24.109 --> 00:01:30.889
Rob Reiner: Well the way I look at any film
that I do is there’s a spine that goes through

00:01:30.889 --> 00:01:36.159
a film and you decide ahead of time what is
the story you want to take?

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:37.759
And in the case of Lyndon, it’s Lyndon.

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:40.639
So this is the story of who is this guy?

00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:45.000
So every scene has to attach to the spine
in some way.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.159
In other ways, each scene you will learn a
little bit more about who this guy is.

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:53.630
So by the end of the journey you say “Okay,
I got a full picture of who he is.”

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.090
Film Courage: You didn’t play a[n acting]
role in this film?

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:02.539
Rob Reiner: You know, I didn't because first
of all I don’t really like acting and directing.

00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:05.499
I find it splits my mind attention, my focus.

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:09.789
I’ve done it but I don’t love it.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:11.379
And there was no real part for me.

00:02:11.379 --> 00:02:12.900
Who am I going to play?

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.230
I can’t be Jack.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.350
I’m too…I’m a Jewish guy from the Bronx.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.830
I can’t be Bobby.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.260
I’m too big.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.750
Film Courage: So you’re known for a fast-paced
directing style.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.440
Would you say it’s sped up over the years
as the world has sped up, or you’ve actually

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.459
slowed down and you’ve been a contrarian?

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:36.569
Rob Reiner: No…I’ve actually have had
to speed it up because I have less time to

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:38.189
make films.

00:02:38.189 --> 00:02:41.239
Every film that I’ve ever made would never
get made in a studio.

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:46.390
They are not the kind of films studios make
so I have to finance them independently and

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.549
whenever you finance a film independently,
you have a strict budget.

00:02:50.549 --> 00:02:53.870
You have a short amount of time to make the
film.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.750
So I love doing crossword puzzles.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.350
I love puzzles and I try to figure out what’s
the best way to get this done in the most

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.900
economic way possible.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:08.470
Film Courage: Having been on both sides of
the camera, from being an actor what were

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:12.439
a few things that you said as a director,
I swear I’m never going to do this.

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:16.260
Whether it’s 50 takes of one scene, not
showing actors dailies, etc.?

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:22.879
Rob Reiner: The main thing I say is I would
never ask an actor to do something I couldn’t

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:23.879
do.

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:28.400
And I’m not the best actor in the world
(I’m a decent actor, but I’m not the best).

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.999
The people who I get, who I ask to play these
parts, they are all better than me at what

00:03:34.999 --> 00:03:37.319
they’re doing, at the part that they are
doing.

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:44.099
So I know if I can do that and make the transition
I ask them to make or play an emotion in the

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:46.431
scene, I know they can do it because I can
do it.

00:03:46.431 --> 00:03:48.840
I would never ask him to do something I couldn’t
do.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.219
Film Courage: I think you’d said 10, 20
years ago you couldn’t do this film.

00:03:53.219 --> 00:03:54.219
Why now?

00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:56.459
Rob Reiner: Well, more than that.

00:03:56.459 --> 00:03:59.080
It was more than 20 years ago.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:06.500
When I think about Lyndon, it was the 60’s
and I was over 18, during this time.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.470
So I didn’t like Lyndon.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:13.580
I mean I saw him one way, as my enemy, the
guy who could send me to have bad things happen

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:18.940
to me, so I didn't like him and it wasn’t
until I matured, I spent time in politics.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:27.000
I had a job in California for seven years,
that I started to really understand and appreciate

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.880
what he was able to accomplish.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.330
And it’s only at that point where I said
“Well, let me take a look at this guy”

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:35.340
because it’s almost like if you look at
it’s like a tale of two terms.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.210
He’s got the overseas invasions and then
he’s got his domestic achievements, which

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.990
(except for the 1930’s) is probably the
greatest of all time and had it not been for

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:48.210
his overseas actions, he probably gone down
as one of the greatest terms of all time.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:52.160
Film Courage: We have a lot of first time
filmmakers who watch our videos.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.750
What would you tell a first time filmmaker
about getting their film made?

00:04:54.750 --> 00:05:02.280
Rob Reiner: Well here’s the beauty…when
I was starting out…you know…making films,

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.060
the materials, that are used to make a film
are expensive.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:12.820
It’s the camera, there’s film stock, there’s
editing, there’s lights (all this stuff).

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:19.889
Now, to make a film, all you need is an iPhone,
a Smart Phone and your computer to edit.

00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:22.230
I mean you can use available light.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.629
So there is no excuse to not just making a
film.

00:05:25.629 --> 00:05:32.240
Write the script, get your friends who you
think can act and actually make a film and

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.699
it doesn’t cost very much.

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.740
So…you know… it’s that…”Just do
it!”

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.770
It’s a Nike thing…[off camera laughter].

